00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.090
at the end for Q&amp;A.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:51.590
All right.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:52.740
Let's dive right in.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.840
So here is how to
frame the problem.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.120
Given a TensorFlow
trained model,

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.590
we would like to optimize
the speed of prediction.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.780
We mean, for a single
example that you

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:09.080
gave to this model for
prediction, optimize

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.150
the time that it takes to
perform that prediction.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.900
And in general, in
a broader sense,

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.270
we would like to
optimize the throughput.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.240
So the number of
examples are inputs per

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.080
second that the system
that's performing the serving

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.550
can process.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:30.200
Also interested, in some cases,
in decreasing model size.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:34.130
Model size has an impact
in lots of things.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:37.950
In many contexts, you care
about the size of your model.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.480
For example, if you're
deploying models to the edge

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.120
or to mobile devices, you
would like those models

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.360
to be as small as possible so
that they download as fast as

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.510
possible, use as little
bandwidth as possible,

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:54.530
that they use a smallest
memory footprint on the device.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:58.400
There are also cases where there
are actual hard limitations

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.200
that you run up against
with respect to model size.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.280
So for example, if you're
using hardware acceleration

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:08.060
to serve your models,
in many cases,

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:09.800
this is limited by
the amount of memory

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.160
on the GPU, the
acceleration device.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:20.000
And in Cloud Machine
Learning Engine,

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.570
which is one of
the methods that we

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:26.040
will talk about later for
serving models, for example,

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:30.110
there is a quota limit
imposed on the model size.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:34.610
I think, by default, it's
something like 512 megabytes

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:35.690
for model size.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.100
Now, that's not a hard limit.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:42.140
You can make a request
and get that increased.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:46.550
But nonetheless, it's something
that you may run into.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:51.950
Your model may end up being
too big to accommodate

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:54.480
whatever serving method that
you're trying to achieve.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.740
So it's important to understand
what factors influence

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.590
the size of your
model, and if there's

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.120
anything to do about it,
what you can do about it.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.930
This just replicates what I
just said about model size.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:09.860
So as you can see, size does
matter with respect to models.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:15.740
So we should be aware of
what factors influence

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:17.600
the size of our models,
and then ultimately,

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.130
what we can do about it.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:22.550
And like I said, prediction
speed, not just important

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.370
when we're talking about
serving a single example.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:28.070
Because in the end, that's
a measure, basically,

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:29.960
of the complexity of
the model, and the time

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:32.350
that it takes to
serve a single example

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:35.930
will be related to the
throughput in the end--

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:37.950
it's a critical factor
in the throughput.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.483
And in turn, that throughput
is related to the amount

00:07:41.483 --> 00:07:43.400
of resources that you're
going to use in order

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.102
to serve your
models, which is then

00:07:45.102 --> 00:07:47.060
related to the amount of
cost that you're going

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.820
to expend to serve models.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:54.860
And relatively small differences
in that speed or throughput

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.370
can have a big impact in
the ROI of your machine

00:07:58.370 --> 00:07:59.900
learning application.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:02.690
So if you can decrease
the amount of resources

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:05.990
necessary to serve your
models by something

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.810
not insignificant,
say, 5% or 10%,

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:11.630
then this should correspondingly
decrease the amount of costs

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:15.570
that it requires to support your
machine learning application,

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:21.510
which can have a big impact in
the ROI of that application.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.080
There's also many cases
where prediction speed

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.240
is essential to the
application itself,

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.348
in cases where we want
to get the result back

00:08:31.348 --> 00:08:33.890
as fast as possible because it
makes a difference in the user

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.860
experience.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:39.530
So we care about prediction
speed, in general,

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.782
and we want to make it
as fast as possible.

00:08:41.782 --> 00:08:43.240
Prediction throughput,
like I said,

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.073
is related to prediction
speed, but includes

00:08:45.073 --> 00:08:49.950
other factors, such as
batching, horizontal scaling,

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.190
meaning how many
parallel resources are

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:57.900
you devoting to serving and
how many examples can you

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.760
process in parallel.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.470
Obviously, network
performance is

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.860
going to have an
impact on throughput.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:06.570
Things that aren't
necessarily related

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:10.500
to TensorFlow or to
machine running at all.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.260
Compute performance,
of course, including

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.110
whether you're using
hardware acceleration or not.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.640
And a bunch of
other stuff that's

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.930
beyond our current scope.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.210
So today, we're really
going to just focus

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:27.000
on how to optimize a trained
TensorFlow model, as I said,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.295
to predict as fast as possible.

00:09:30.295 --> 00:09:32.170
And then, of course,
the other stuff matters,

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:35.310
but that's beyond
our current scope.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.070
So let's talk a little bit
more about the TensorFlow model

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:42.410
formats and how they are
implicated in all this.

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:45.180
So there are more than
what you see here.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:49.740
Here, I'm showing two
formats, the graph def

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.490
and the saved model.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:54.567
You probably are familiar
with the saved model

00:09:54.567 --> 00:09:56.900
if you've trained TensorFlow
models, because this is now

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:01.400
the most common model format
that, for example, when

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:04.130
you train a model
using an estimator

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.500
and you export that model,
it's going to be exported

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.420
as a saved model.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:12.320
The saved model actually
includes a graph def

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:19.700
plus a set of saved weights,
basically, as files in a folder

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:20.480
structure.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:22.640
So a saved model is
not a single file,

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:26.270
it's a folder that
contains the definition

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:29.030
of your graph in this
graph def format,

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:31.880
and in addition,
some weights that

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.440
are in a separate set of
files that are merged together

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:40.280
with that graph when you
are serving the model.

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:45.800
The graph def is a either
text or binary file

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:52.070
format that uses the
protobuf file format

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.750
or the protobuf data structure
format, which you probably

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:55.500
are familiar with.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:00.750
It's very ubiquitous across
everything that Google does.

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:04.790
And so it could be, again,
either in text or in binary.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:08.000
But it basically represents
all of the node structure

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.650
of your graph.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:14.870
So any TensorFlow model can
be expressed as a graph,

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:17.270
and the graph def
just really encodes

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:19.248
the structure of that graph.

00:11:19.248 --> 00:11:21.290
So again, the saved model
consists of a graph def

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:23.540
plus a set of weights.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:28.490
Actually, more accurate to say
that the saved model includes

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:30.810
what's called a meta graph.

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:36.240
So this is a graph def that
includes some extra information

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:42.150
about what we call the serving
signature of the model, which

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:45.640
is the inputs and
outputs of the model.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:51.360
So a saved model is a
meta graph plus a bunch

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.713
of weights stored in separate
files or folder structure.

00:11:54.713 --> 00:11:56.130
This is all very
important to know

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:59.880
when we're trying to
operate on these models

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.920
and try to optimize
them, because we

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.330
have to understand what the
underlying data structures are.

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:11.920
But we'll talk about several
basic optimization techniques,

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:17.210
including freezing
the graph, pruning

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:21.310
the graph, something
called constant folding,

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:27.377
an operation called folding
batch norms, and quantization.

00:12:27.377 --> 00:12:29.710
And there are actually many
other kinds of optimizations

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:31.320
that can be done.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:32.590
Including a few of them here.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:38.710
We're not going to go into
gory detail about any of these,

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:41.620
but these are good
ones to know about

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:44.210
for optimization techniques.

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:46.000
And as I mentioned,
actually, there

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:48.580
are components of the
TensorFlow runtime, which

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.340
we will talk about that perform
many of these optimizations

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:52.660
as well.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:58.370
Tools that we will use
are really two main ones.

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:00.290
There's something
called a graph transform

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.790
tool, which has been
in the TensorFlow code

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:07.900
base for some time, the
world of TensorFlow for eons,

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:11.260
since 2016.

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:13.390
And that in the blog post,
and then the examples

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:17.440
that I'm going to cover is the
main tool that we will use.

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:21.520
However, there is another
tool called grappler

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:23.870
that is present in the
TensorFlow runtime that

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:25.930
is performing a lot of
these optimizations,

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.320
again, for-- it should be, in
a theoretical sense, for you

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:32.150
automatically, such that you
don't have to think about it.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.150
The reality is a little
bit different than that.

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:37.190
We'll talk more about
why that is the case.

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:40.630
The graph transform tool, by
the way, is a command line tool,

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:43.150
but it also has a
Python interface.

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:46.240
Grappler is basically a
component of the TensorFlow

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:53.500
runtime that recently
also got a Python

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:55.340
API associated with it as well.

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:57.130
So you can call
both of these things

00:13:57.130 --> 00:14:00.560
through Python code, which
is what we will do here.

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.770
But in the case of the GTT,
the graph transform tool,

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:06.020
there's also just a command line
version of that you can use.

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:09.400
So in summary, the
optimization process is this.

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:11.800
We're going to take a
saved model, which, again,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:16.270
is by far the most common
format that you probably

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:19.270
are dealing with when you've
got a trained TensorFlow

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:21.190
model that's been exported.

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:23.890
We're going to convert
that into a graph def.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:27.100
And that process is
called freezing the model.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:32.560
Basically, merging the weights
that are saved out externally

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.590
with the graph, such
that it now is all

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:37.300
contained in a single file.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:42.460
Why would we need to do that
before we start optimizing?

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:46.150
The answer is that if you
keep the weights separate

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:48.700
from the graph and you start
operating on the graph,

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.010
pruning it and changing
it and optimizing it,

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:53.850
then that structure
that existed before

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:57.130
and the weights is no longer
going to map back to the graph,

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.620
and you won't be able to
upload those weights back in.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.570
You might be removing
nodes, you might

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.670
be collapsing nodes together.

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:06.980
And so, effectively,
if you don't do this,

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:09.380
you will have a broken model.

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:13.450
So really, this model freezing
step is the first step in any

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:17.760
optimization process, so
that when you're dealing with

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:21.970
a graph that contains all of the
weights merged into the graph

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.520
as opposed to stored separately,
so that then when we operate

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:27.580
on that graph, again, pruning
nodes and merging nodes

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:30.220
together and changing the
structure of the graph,

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.640
that we don't-- a structure
now that no longer--

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:38.067
where the weights and the graph
don't have the same structure.

00:15:38.067 --> 00:15:40.150
Once we've done that, once
we've frozen the model,

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:41.950
then we can optimize
the model by basically

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:45.940
operating on that graph, again,
doing all kinds of operations

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:49.780
that we'll talk about to the
graph that involve shrinking

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:53.480
the graph size and making the
graph more efficient, such

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:56.710
that it contains
fewer operations--

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:58.420
optimizing it.

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:00.640
And then once we've
done that, we're

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:04.180
going to want to convert
that back to a saved model,

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.250
because in the end,
the saved model

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:08.240
is the most useful model format.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:10.210
So if you're going to
be loading this model

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:13.985
into a serving process, like
TensorFlow Serving or a cloud

00:16:13.985 --> 00:16:16.360
machine running engine, it's
going to want a saved model.

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:18.440
Not the case that
when you do that,

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:22.450
you somehow re-export those
weights out to separate files.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:28.150
So a saved model can
consist of a graph

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:31.000
file that has all of the
weights merged into it,

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:33.040
and then just an empty
set of directories

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:37.660
that contain nothing, and
TensorFlow is fine with that.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:42.460
But we still need to add
this meta graph information

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:46.510
that I mentioned
back to the graph def

00:16:46.510 --> 00:16:52.363
to convert it from a graph
def into a saved model graph.

00:16:52.363 --> 00:16:54.280
But we don't need to be
re-export the weights,

00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:56.650
so the weights will still
be merged into this--

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:58.810
it'll still be a
single file, basically,

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:01.390
just a saved model
folder structure

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:06.500
that has just this one file in
it that represents the model.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:08.868
So let's talk a little
bit more in detail

00:17:08.868 --> 00:17:09.910
about freezing the model.

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:12.700
Again, this says this
basically consists

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:15.700
of merging the weights
into the graph,

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:17.868
giving you a single file.

00:17:17.868 --> 00:17:18.910
I mentioned that already.

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:20.035
I covered that pretty well.

00:17:20.035 --> 00:17:24.069
So pruning is another
optimization process

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:26.560
that we'll talk about
that essentially consists

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:30.790
of a set of operations that
reduce the size of the graph,

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:34.930
either removing extra nodes
that aren't necessary--

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:37.480
we call, for example,
identity nodes, meaning,

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:41.290
nodes that act like the
multiplication of one,

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:45.850
or basically, they're like no-op
nodes that don't do anything

00:17:45.850 --> 00:17:49.750
to the graph or don't affect
the computation that's

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:51.460
being performed by the graph.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.440
Other things that--
operations that you

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:56.335
might do in TensorFlow,
like shuffling on a tensor

00:17:56.335 --> 00:18:01.210
of size 1 or reduction of
dimensions, empty dimensions,

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:03.850
and things that happen in the
course of model development

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:08.830
or in the course of operating
on your TensorFlow model

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:12.310
that the optimization
process can eliminate,

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:16.308
which then results in a
smaller, more efficient graph.

00:18:16.308 --> 00:18:18.100
In addition, a lot of
the times when you're

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:21.700
developing a graph in TensorFlow
through some of the APIs,

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:27.460
there will be multiple output
nodes created, meaning--

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:29.470
and an output node
is basically a node

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:32.270
of the graph that
produces the answer.

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:34.270
And so it's not always
the case that you

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:37.240
have one set of outputs for
a TensorFlow graph, sometimes

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.400
you have multiple outputs.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:42.160
But it's almost always the
case that when you're actually

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:46.520
using the graph, you only
care about one of the outputs.

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:51.143
And so when you actually use the
graph in a production context,

00:18:51.143 --> 00:18:53.560
then you want to make sure
that none of those other output

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:55.703
nodes are present in the
graph, because that's

00:18:55.703 --> 00:18:57.370
just extra computation
that the graph is

00:18:57.370 --> 00:18:59.600
doing that is not necessary.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:03.100
So we want to make sure that
we remove extra outputs.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:04.600
It's not always the
case that you'll

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:06.910
have these extra outputs,
but there are some APIs

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:11.170
that when you use them
produce multiple outputs.

00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:14.440
It's possible that when you
define your graph, however

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:17.680
you're doing that, using
the high level API,

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:19.870
that you would get
multiple output nodes.

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:24.370
And so this is one of the
things that we had to prune.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:30.970
So constant folding is basically
merging constant operations

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:34.960
that might be chained together
and just pre-computing them.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:38.260
Using batch norms is
actually similar to this.

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:42.870
So I don't know-- who's familiar
with batch normalization?

00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:43.940
A few of you.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.940
If you're not familiar
with batch normalization,

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:49.330
I would say you should be.

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:56.613
It's becoming-- I would call
it a standard technique that

00:19:56.613 --> 00:19:58.780
may have been a research
technique a couple of years

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:06.340
ago, but by now, anybody who's
doing serious deep learning

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:10.670
should be using batch
norm inside their models.

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:15.610
In fact, as of, I
think, TensorFlow 1.10,

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:18.625
they included batch
normalization in the canned

00:20:18.625 --> 00:20:21.100
estimators of TensorFlow.

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:23.640
So it's actually
all built into the--

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:27.730
if you're going to specify a
deep neural net using the DNN

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:32.128
estimator classes, you have the
option to turn batch norm on.

00:20:32.128 --> 00:20:33.670
So again, it's almost
always the case

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:36.030
that you actually
want to do batch norm.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.260
There's not really time to
talk about that in detail,

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:39.220
what batch norm is.

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:41.523
But trust me, if
you're not using it

00:20:41.523 --> 00:20:42.940
and you have a
deep neural network

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:45.550
or a convolutional neural
net, you should be.

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:50.200
Effectively, what it does is
normalizes the activations

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:53.110
of each layer as the layers--

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:56.230
as those activation values
propagate up through the model

00:20:56.230 --> 00:21:00.370
by looking at the statistics
of the data, basically.

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.980
Starting with the input
data, namely, the batches

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:06.260
of data that you're
feeding into the model,

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:08.320
but even as you go up
through the layers, there's

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:12.190
a computation that's
being done so that--

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:16.660
by normalizing, meaning
subtracting away the mean

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:19.420
and centering those values.

00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:24.850
This turns out to make learning
much easier and faster.

00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:28.330
But in the end, you're computing
some statistics on those inputs

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:30.400
and on the values of
those activations,

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:34.910
and those statistics are
pre-computed during training,

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:37.070
they're not computed
during predictions.

00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:41.140
So the statistics are
computed as a mean

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:44.420
and a standard deviation
during the training process,

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:46.690
and then they're embedded
as constant values

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:48.320
inside the network.

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:52.720
And you can just merge those
constant multiplications

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:55.358
that are happening there
into the graph itself.

00:21:55.358 --> 00:21:57.775
So that's a lot of explanation
about what using batch norm

00:21:57.775 --> 00:22:00.130
is, but it does give
us the opportunity

00:22:00.130 --> 00:22:03.615
to talk about batch
normalization and what it is

00:22:03.615 --> 00:22:04.990
and why you should
know about it.

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:07.410
Yeah?

00:22:29.663 --> 00:22:31.080
I guess the answer
to the question

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:36.120
is yes, that we are explicitly
doing operations to a model

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:37.980
to prepare it for serving.

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:42.600
And if you wanted to, say, do
further training on that model,

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:44.790
say, incremental training
or add more training,

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:46.380
that, yeah, you would
not want to have

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:48.450
this fused optimized model.

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:50.910
Instead, you would want
to start with a model that

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:55.350
still had the batch
normalization structure in it.

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:57.870
So yeah.

00:22:57.870 --> 00:22:59.610
We're really talking
about a process

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:04.320
that we apply to models after
they're trained to prepare them

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:05.190
for serving.

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:12.895
Yeah, that's right.

00:23:12.895 --> 00:23:14.680
In other words, we're
computing statistics

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:17.350
based upon an exponentially
weighted average

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:18.460
of the previous values.

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:20.980
And again, we don't need
to go into gory detail

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:22.700
about what batch norm
is or how it works.

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:25.420
But you should at least--

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:29.360
the concept or the name
should be familiar to you.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:33.770
I advise you, if you don't
know what it is, learn about it

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:35.320
and learn how you--

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:37.420
what you should know or
what you should make sure

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:41.170
is that it's on
inside your model.

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:46.270
Hopefully, the
frameworks-- certainly

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:49.155
Keras will do this for you, as
well as some of these canned

00:23:49.155 --> 00:23:49.690
estimators.

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:52.600
But for those of us that
are building custom models,

00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:55.570
we have to explicitly include
batch norm inside our model

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.310
definition.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:00.850
Quantization is quite a
big topic in and of itself,

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:05.380
but essentially refers to taking
the weights inside a model

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:07.150
and reducing their precision.

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:11.140
So going from a 32-bit weight,
in some cases, a 64-bit weight,

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:16.150
and reducing down to a
16-bit or an 8-bit weight.

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:22.000
This does reduce the accuracy
of the model in many cases,

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:25.300
but not by much.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:28.960
And you can get a really big
boost in speed and performance

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:32.530
by quantizing, and you get
a much, much reduced model

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:33.970
size as well.

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:38.860
Because most of the size of a
model is due to its weights.

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:41.380
And so if you can reduce
the amount of data

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:44.530
that it takes to store those
weights by a factor of four,

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:46.090
you get a much,
much smaller model.

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:49.090
Quantization is
really implicated

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:52.540
in a lot of what we do with
respect to model optimization

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:54.100
these days.

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:57.430
Again, for the time, you
don't have to really know

00:24:57.430 --> 00:24:59.290
about the details of this.

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:00.620
It's done for you.

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:03.670
But there are cases
where you really

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:06.890
have to understand quantization
and how to apply it.

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:09.310
And if you do that,
you can get really big

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:11.870
boosts in performance,
certainly in speed,

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:13.300
but also in model size.

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:18.070
Now, at a cost, by the way,
of some amount of accuracy.

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:20.805
So if you're going to apply
quantization to your model--

00:25:20.805 --> 00:25:22.930
actually, really, this
applies to all of the things

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:24.100
that we talked about here--

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:27.490
you should test before and
after to make sure that nothing

00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:30.670
that you did changes
your accuracy

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:33.940
or the precision
of your results,

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:39.170
at least not in a way that makes
your use case no longer valid.

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:42.040
Again, this is what
the transformation

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:44.710
looks like when you're doing
quantization inside a graph.

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:50.260
You're taking something that is
a simple sequence of floating

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:51.740
point operations--

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:54.895
so here, we have an input
where a relu activation

00:25:54.895 --> 00:25:56.770
function is being applied
to it that produces

00:25:56.770 --> 00:25:58.960
an output, and instead--

00:25:58.960 --> 00:26:01.300
because, again, you're
reducing the precision,

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:05.500
and so you need to know what
the range is of that weight

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:07.540
so that you can map it back.

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:09.910
So you have to
pre-compute, which

00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:13.180
the system will do for you or
the optimization tools will do

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:15.382
for you, the min and the max.

00:26:15.382 --> 00:26:17.840
Then you reduce the precision,
you perform the calculation,

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:20.995
and then you map it back to the
original floating point value.

00:26:20.995 --> 00:26:25.060
And folks heard about
TensorFlow Lite.

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:27.890
So this is a system
introduced last year,

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:32.740
basically, to optimize the
deployment of TensorFlow models

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:34.190
on mobile devices.

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:36.580
One of the key techniques
that TensorFlow Lite is doing

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:39.610
to make models work well
and fast on mobile devices

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:42.722
is quantizing down to 8 bits.

00:26:42.722 --> 00:26:44.430
So in this case,
TensorFlow Lite actually

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:45.888
does all this
quantization for you.

00:26:45.888 --> 00:26:48.100
You don't have to
worry about it.

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:51.930
But that is the secret sauce
under a lot of what TensorFlow

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:54.210
Lite is doing, is basically
quantizing your model

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:55.680
down to 8-bit.

00:26:55.680 --> 00:26:58.780
I talked a fair bit about all
the various optimizations.

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:02.440
Let's look in more
detail at an example.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:06.150
We're just going to talk about
a MNIST convolutional neural

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:12.850
net, which is a standard neural
net that you might deal with.

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:15.510
And this is the full
optimization process

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:17.220
that we will apply.

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:24.870
First, we will run predictions
so that we know the model

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:28.830
output before we applied
all over optimizations

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:31.410
and we know how fast it ran.

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:33.240
So we have to do that
before so that we

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:34.920
have some kind of
benchmark or baseline

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:37.890
so that we know what effect
our optimizations had.

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:40.620
We also want to run
predictions so that we

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:44.860
can verify, as I said, which
we should always do at the end,

00:27:44.860 --> 00:27:47.430
that we didn't break
our model or do anything

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:52.080
that would reduce the
precision of our model

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:53.310
or broke the output.

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:55.830
It's quite easy, frankly,
to break your model

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:57.340
by doing this stuff to it.

00:27:57.340 --> 00:27:59.400
And so you want to
make sure that you

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:02.550
can verify that the model
at the end of the process

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:06.105
still performs well, or at
least in an accurate way.

00:28:06.105 --> 00:28:07.480
And then you also
want to be able

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:09.210
quantify the effect
of the optimizations.

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:13.390
So you need to have a baseline
benchmark at the beginning.

00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:16.970
Then you freeze the graph,
you run the optimizations.

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:21.350
Again, we're going to show this
using the graph transform tool,

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:26.060
but there's also this, actually,
more advanced and more recent

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:29.330
tool called grappler
that you can also use.

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:32.580
We'll convert that model
back to a saved model format,

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:34.220
and then we rerun
the predictions

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:37.430
to verify that we
didn't break it,

00:28:37.430 --> 00:28:39.930
that it's still as accurate
as we need it to be.

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:42.230
And we also benchmark
at that point

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:45.170
to quantify the effect
of our optimizations.

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:48.630
Let me just say a few words
about benchmarking methodology.

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.090
There could be a whole
separate talk on that,

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:53.220
but it's really
important, I think,

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:57.330
to be careful about
how we benchmark.

00:28:57.330 --> 00:28:59.070
Because if we're
not careful, then we

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:01.920
may draw some
incorrect conclusions

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:03.870
about what we just did.

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:05.910
We may decide that,
oh, these optimizations

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:10.770
didn't work at all because it
makes the prediction longer.

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:15.300
And so it's important to have
some consistent benchmarking

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:19.400
methodology and to pay
attention to what we do here.

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:22.260
And again, like I said, I won't
go into gory detail about this,

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:26.280
but I just want to say that
the three desirable attributes

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:28.800
of a benchmark are that
it's repeatable, meaning,

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:31.020
that you get the
same answer when

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:32.970
you run it multiple times--

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:38.520
oftentimes, because
computers are a little bit

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:43.860
indeterministic,
frankly, in varying ways,

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:46.630
you often won't get necessarily
the same exact results.

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:49.080
So when we say repeatable,
we're not necessarily

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:50.730
saying you get the
exact same result,

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:54.000
but it needs to be within
a small range, needs

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.250
to have low variance.

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:58.870
We want to run the benchmarks
in a controlled environment,

00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:02.040
meaning, you have to
have a lot of control

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:05.550
about all of the different
components that we're testing.

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:07.470
If there's some part
of that component

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:10.680
that we don't control, say,
for example, the network--

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:14.010
if you're running a
benchmark from your laptop

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:18.690
across the cloud to something
that's running in the cloud,

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:20.370
you've got a big chunk
of stuff that you

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:22.920
have no control over
there between you

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:24.532
and what you're running.

00:30:24.532 --> 00:30:26.490
And we also want to make
sure the benchmark has

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:27.948
a limited focus,
meaning, it's only

00:30:27.948 --> 00:30:30.360
testing the things that
we're interested in

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:34.710
and not trying to test
lots of different things.

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:38.610
Because, again, that reduces the
amount of control that we have.

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:42.690
By definition, testing more
things makes it more likely

00:30:42.690 --> 00:30:44.770
that there's some
part of that system.

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:47.800
So again, that's all I
want to say about that.

00:30:47.800 --> 00:30:51.180
But it influences
the choice of how

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.790
we might decide to
benchmark our model

00:30:53.790 --> 00:30:55.860
optimizations because
there are multiple ways

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:57.060
that we could do that.

00:30:57.060 --> 00:31:00.690
So one of them would be
just to use pure Python.

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:02.790
It's a very easy thing
to do now, particularly

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:06.100
with this contra
predictor module that

00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:08.620
was added to TensorFlow
that allows you to perform

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:10.120
predictions in Python.

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:11.770
Now, of course, this
is TensorFlow 1.0

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:15.105
that has the contrib
package, which is going away.

00:31:15.105 --> 00:31:16.380
AUDIENCE: It's migrated--

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:17.825
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Migrating.

00:31:17.825 --> 00:31:18.950
It's not going away, right.

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:20.492
Yeah, that's the
wrong way to say it.

00:31:20.492 --> 00:31:24.670
It's migrating to somewhere else
where it will be still useful--

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:26.390
still be able to be used.

00:31:26.390 --> 00:31:28.570
Anyway, that's a
potential method.

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:31.300
Cloud Machine Learning
Engine predict service--

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:34.360
very useful, important
service, which I'll

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:36.010
talk a little bit
more about later.

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:38.680
Very, very useful for
production prediction.

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:41.140
You can train your models on
Cloud Machine Learning Engine,

00:31:41.140 --> 00:31:44.470
and then you can very
easily take that same model

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:48.520
and set up a predict
service for it on the cloud.

00:31:48.520 --> 00:31:51.520
And then another option
would be TF serving, which

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:52.855
is another serving option.

00:31:52.855 --> 00:31:54.730
Again, I will actually
talk a little bit more

00:31:54.730 --> 00:31:59.250
about all the various
serving options later using

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:00.280
just a REST call.

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:02.180
So basically,
running an instance

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:05.220
a TF serving in a
container locally.

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:11.640
Of these three, can anybody
say which is the best?

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:17.470
So the answer is
TF serving locally.

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:19.900
The Python method
suffers from the fact

00:32:19.900 --> 00:32:23.620
that Python itself is not a
very controlled environment

00:32:23.620 --> 00:32:26.425
because it's an interpreter
that is doing a lot of things

00:32:26.425 --> 00:32:28.300
under the hood that you
have no control over,

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:29.710
like garbage collection.

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:34.360
And it tends to just be very
non-deterministic from run

00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:35.080
to run.

00:32:35.080 --> 00:32:38.980
If you try running
your code using Python,

00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:42.280
even if you do make
it more statistically

00:32:42.280 --> 00:32:45.750
valid by doing bigger
batches or more predictions,

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:50.670
you will find that the
results are quite variable.

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:52.310
You could overcome this.

00:32:52.310 --> 00:32:56.540
You could run the benchmark 10
times and average the results.

00:32:56.540 --> 00:32:58.100
But you'd rather
not have to do that.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:00.912
Cloud Machine Learning
Engine, as we said,

00:33:00.912 --> 00:33:03.370
the challenge is to see you
running something in the cloud,

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:06.420
and you're trying to
test that locally,

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:09.010
or even if you're having an
instance on the cloud that's

00:33:09.010 --> 00:33:11.350
making calls out to
Machine Learning Engine

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:13.450
to do that predict,
there's a lot of stuff

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:15.610
there that you just
have no control over.

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:18.890
And Machine Learning Engine
predict is a managed service,

00:33:18.890 --> 00:33:20.980
so that means it's handling
a lot of the scaling

00:33:20.980 --> 00:33:21.785
issues for you.

00:33:21.785 --> 00:33:23.410
So in the middle of
your benchmark run,

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:26.852
it might see that
your load is spiking

00:33:26.852 --> 00:33:28.810
and try to allocate some
more instances for you

00:33:28.810 --> 00:33:30.130
to do this prediction,
which would then

00:33:30.130 --> 00:33:32.440
throw your benchmark results
off completely and not

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:33.920
be very repeatable.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:36.210
So not a great choice.

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:38.410
Running TF serving locally
in a docker container

00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:41.270
is actually quite controlled.

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:44.620
And it should be quite
consistent from run to run,

00:33:44.620 --> 00:33:47.868
because, again, you're
running it on your laptop,

00:33:47.868 --> 00:33:49.660
you've got a lot of
other processes running

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:51.910
at the same time, and that
could influence the result.

00:33:51.910 --> 00:33:54.550
But nonetheless, of
those three options,

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:57.140
it's, I would claim, the best.

00:33:57.140 --> 00:33:59.640
And so that's the one that
we used in the blog post.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:01.540
Again, in the code
that's out on GitHub,

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:05.600
you will see all three
options as examples,

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:08.145
so you could test
each of them out.

00:34:08.145 --> 00:34:09.520
And it's also just
useful to have

00:34:09.520 --> 00:34:14.150
sample code that uses the contra
predictor to do predictions.

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:16.449
You may not have even
known that that existed,

00:34:16.449 --> 00:34:18.550
the Cloud Machine
Learning Engine.

00:34:18.550 --> 00:34:20.710
Also useful to have
an example of that.

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:25.420
And then finally, the
deploying and using TF

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:28.630
serving in a local container
as well is also useful.

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:31.150
I'm going to make a little
diversion here quickly

00:34:31.150 --> 00:34:32.980
to talk about the
various options

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:36.139
for serving TensorFlow in
a production environment.

00:34:36.139 --> 00:34:38.294
We've talked about Cloud
Machine Learning Engine,

00:34:38.294 --> 00:34:40.850
the predict service thereof.

00:34:40.850 --> 00:34:42.210
It's a managed service.

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:45.330
A lot of great features
for doing prediction.

00:34:45.330 --> 00:34:48.310
A very easy transition
from training to predict.

00:34:48.310 --> 00:34:51.370
Basically, in your training
process, at the end of it,

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:54.159
you export the saved
model, and then it's

00:34:54.159 --> 00:34:56.409
very easy to launch
a predict service

00:34:56.409 --> 00:34:58.540
from that same saved model.

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:01.180
You can do online predictions
and batch predictions.

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:04.930
It contains facilities
to do model versioning.

00:35:04.930 --> 00:35:08.530
It does support other
frameworks than TensorFlow now.

00:35:08.530 --> 00:35:10.600
So Scikit-Learn and XGBoost.

00:35:10.600 --> 00:35:13.610
And I was asked by the product
manager or the tech lead

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:16.810
here to mention that
very soon, you'll

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:19.900
be able to deploy predict
on any machine type that's

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:21.460
available in GCE.

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:25.780
So you'll have even more control
over the kinds of machines

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:30.230
that you're running on,
and also to harness GPUs.

00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:34.430
So really good
option for deploying

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:38.335
your models and the
predictions, again,

00:35:38.335 --> 00:35:40.460
if you're interested in a
managed service, meaning,

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:45.080
you don't need to have
very strong control

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:47.450
over the underlying
architecture.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:52.010
Very good for deploying your
models to production, not so

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:54.630
great as a benchmarking
tool necessarily.

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:56.180
TF serving we talked about.

00:35:56.180 --> 00:36:01.220
Based on a open source
component from the TFX platform,

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:05.090
which is Google's internal
platform for machine learning

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:09.173
pipelines, which we are now open
sourcing the components of one

00:36:09.173 --> 00:36:09.840
after the other.

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:13.220
I believe the serving component
was the first component that

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:16.285
was open sourced.

00:36:16.285 --> 00:36:17.910
One of the nice things
about TF serving

00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:22.080
is that it has the facility to
update the models dynamically,

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:27.480
meaning, it can be up and
running, serving your examples,

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:30.815
and then you swap in a
new model under the hood

00:36:30.815 --> 00:36:32.190
and TF serving
will automatically

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:36.270
pick that up and just seamlessly
transition from the old model

00:36:36.270 --> 00:36:37.740
to the new one.

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:40.620
There's a large community
inside Google and outside Google

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:42.710
that's using TF serving.

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:47.640
I will say, if
you're using GPUs,

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:50.888
it is challenging to harness
multiple GPUs using TF serving.

00:36:50.888 --> 00:36:53.430
It can be done, but you have to
encode the device assignments

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:54.000
in the graph.

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:57.280
And so that's one, I would
say, drawback of TF serving.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:37:01.200
If you really have stringent
throughput requirements

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:03.450
for your serving
application and you really

00:37:03.450 --> 00:37:05.700
need to have running
on machines that

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:10.090
have four or eight GPUs
attached with them,

00:37:10.090 --> 00:37:15.080
then it's a challenging
thing to do with TF serving.

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:19.960
The architecture for a
serving system and production

00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:22.480
looks like this, where
you typically would deploy

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:26.080
TF serving inside a Kubernetes
cluster, where each node has

00:37:26.080 --> 00:37:27.790
its own instance of TF serving.

00:37:27.790 --> 00:37:30.040
And then you have some kind
of load balancer in front

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:33.370
that is distributing
those serving requests

00:37:33.370 --> 00:37:34.640
across the nodes.

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:38.110
There's an option from
our partner NVIDIA called

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:42.280
NVIDIA Inference Server,
which includes the capability

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:44.800
to harness something
called TensorRT, which I'll

00:37:44.800 --> 00:37:49.000
talk about a little more,
that basically harnesses

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:54.760
some of the capabilities
of the newer NVIDIA GPUs.

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:58.270
They have something called a
tensor core unit inside them.

00:37:58.270 --> 00:38:02.710
Actually, this tensor core unit
that is used under TensorRT

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:04.532
is basically harnessing
quantization.

00:38:04.532 --> 00:38:06.490
So under the hood, it's
converting your weights

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:10.180
into 16-bit and it's got
hardware acceleration.

00:38:10.180 --> 00:38:14.830
It's really, really fast for
processing with these weights.

00:38:14.830 --> 00:38:20.170
This feature is present in
the V100 and the new P4, which

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:23.950
we just announced publicly
that's available on Google

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:26.030
Cloud for serving.

00:38:26.030 --> 00:38:31.220
It's a view core that's
really specialized to serving.

00:38:31.220 --> 00:38:33.820
So it doesn't have a
lot of onboard memory,

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:35.950
but it's really fast
and cheap, or cheaper

00:38:35.950 --> 00:38:38.530
than some of the other
options for doing serving.

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:41.180
It's also, like I said,
present in the V100.

00:38:41.180 --> 00:38:41.680
OK.

00:38:41.680 --> 00:38:43.960
So back up to the
optimization process.

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:46.750
I talked about running
predictions and benchmarking

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:47.393
the baseline.

00:38:47.393 --> 00:38:49.060
I think I actually
had a number up there

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:54.430
that was 189 seconds for
this particular MNIST CNN,

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:57.160
simple MNIST CNN, which
is, basically, I think,

00:38:57.160 --> 00:39:03.130
1,000 executions of
a batch size of 100.

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:05.950
This is what it looks
like to freeze the graph.

00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:09.370
As I said, we're calling
this freeze graph operation

00:39:09.370 --> 00:39:15.500
using the Python API, which
is present in the Python tools

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:17.990
module under TensorFlow.

00:39:17.990 --> 00:39:21.920
This freeze graph tool can
be called directly here.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:25.382
It actually has
one of the largest

00:39:25.382 --> 00:39:26.840
set of arguments
of any method call

00:39:26.840 --> 00:39:28.350
that I've seen in TensorFlow.

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:29.600
I didn't put all of them here.

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:32.660
There's, like, 10 more
that are required.

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:34.100
They're not even optional.

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:37.420
But anyway, the sample code
shows you how to do this.

00:39:37.420 --> 00:39:39.950
And then the output
is basically--

00:39:39.950 --> 00:39:42.140
you're providing the
output graph file name,

00:39:42.140 --> 00:39:43.925
and so it actually
writes the output graph

00:39:43.925 --> 00:39:48.980
to the frozen output graph
for you when it completes.

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:50.990
This is what running
the optimizations

00:39:50.990 --> 00:39:53.330
using the graph transform
tool looks like.

00:39:53.330 --> 00:39:56.300
Again, you give it a list of the
tech transforms that you want

00:39:56.300 --> 00:40:00.650
to run, then you run this
transform graph method,

00:40:00.650 --> 00:40:05.645
and it produces the optimized
graph as the output.

00:40:05.645 --> 00:40:07.520
Then you have to actually
go save that graph.

00:40:07.520 --> 00:40:11.890
But again, the sample code
shows you how to do all that.

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:15.800
And then finally, you want to
convert back to saved model.

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:17.720
This turns out to be
unwieldy and harder

00:40:17.720 --> 00:40:19.280
than you'd like it to be.

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:22.020
But basically, if you know
the right APIs to call,

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:23.680
it's not that bad.

00:40:23.680 --> 00:40:29.670
You use the TF saved
model simple save method.

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:33.340
And again, you have to
provide the inputs--

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:36.590
a list of inputs and the list of
outputs that you want to save.

00:40:36.590 --> 00:40:38.360
And so you have to
actually know that

00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:40.390
or know what those nodes are.

00:40:40.390 --> 00:40:42.802
In this example code,
we found the inputs

00:40:42.802 --> 00:40:45.260
by just looking for a place
holder nodes, which essentially

00:40:45.260 --> 00:40:50.080
are typically what would
provide the inputs to a graph.

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:53.590
We got the outputs, actually, by
looking at the meta graph def,

00:40:53.590 --> 00:40:54.610
as I talked about.

00:40:54.610 --> 00:40:56.470
The meta graph def
includes the signature

00:40:56.470 --> 00:40:58.960
that gives a list
of the output nodes,

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:03.470
and so that's where
that came from.

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:07.210
And then the benchmark again,
after applying these set

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:11.140
of optimizations, and
instead of 189 seconds,

00:41:11.140 --> 00:41:12.950
it takes 162 seconds.

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:16.490
So that's about a
15% improvement,

00:41:16.490 --> 00:41:19.360
which is not bad for just
running a few optimizations.

00:41:19.360 --> 00:41:20.860
And it's actually
significant enough

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:24.130
that it could make a
difference in cost and speed,

00:41:24.130 --> 00:41:27.520
certainly, a difference
that you would care about.

00:41:27.520 --> 00:41:29.590
Each of the optimizations
that we perform

00:41:29.590 --> 00:41:34.400
should preserve the
output for a given input.

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:36.220
In other words,
hopefully-- again, this

00:41:36.220 --> 00:41:39.400
is something we have to verify,
but there shouldn't be anything

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:44.386
that we did that would change
the output for a given input.

00:41:44.386 --> 00:41:44.886
Yep?

00:41:44.886 --> 00:41:47.520
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
the quantization on it?

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:48.937
LUKMAN RAMSEY:
Actually, we didn't

00:41:48.937 --> 00:41:51.580
do quantization this one.

00:41:51.580 --> 00:41:52.140
Sorry--

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:53.820
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:54.662
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yes.

00:41:54.662 --> 00:42:00.450
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:03.360
LUKMAN RAMSEY: So TensorRT
actually only makes sense

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:05.485
if you're doing it on GPU.

00:42:05.485 --> 00:42:07.110
In fact, really, by
definition, what it

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:09.900
is is preparing your
model so that it

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:12.660
is able to harness those
tensor cores that are

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:15.000
built into the V100 and the P4.

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:18.630
And I think all of the GPUs
going forward from NVIDIA,

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:22.950
or most of them, will
include this special unit.

00:42:22.950 --> 00:42:25.090
TensorRT is a topic
in and of itself.

00:42:25.090 --> 00:42:26.590
We can talk a little
bit about that.

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:28.465
There is sample code
out there that shows you

00:42:28.465 --> 00:42:30.000
how to apply TensorRT.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.670
Again, it's been present
inside the TensorFlow code base

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:37.950
since, I think, March
of last year, as of 1.7.

00:42:37.950 --> 00:42:40.050
So it's reasonably stable.

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:43.350
But you also need to
use the NVIDIA Inference

00:42:43.350 --> 00:42:45.610
Server in order to harness it.

00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:49.350
Having said that,
if you really want

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:54.480
really high performing serving,
it's pretty much the way to go.

00:42:54.480 --> 00:42:56.400
I also would say
you probably would

00:42:56.400 --> 00:43:00.410
need to engage with NVIDIA
in order to make it work.

00:43:00.410 --> 00:43:03.000
It's complicated
to get it to work.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:06.320
We're just about to publish a--

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:08.700
actually, there's already been
a blog post that shows you

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:10.560
how to do this on V100s.

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:15.480
Just about to publish another
blog post that shows you

00:43:15.480 --> 00:43:17.280
how to do it using the P4s.

00:43:17.280 --> 00:43:19.680
So you'll have sample code
out there available if you're

00:43:19.680 --> 00:43:20.790
interested in doing this.

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:30.620
But like I said, it's
something that you probably

00:43:30.620 --> 00:43:31.282
need the help--

00:43:31.282 --> 00:43:33.740
you'll probably end up needing
some technical help in order

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:36.355
to pull it off.

00:43:36.355 --> 00:43:37.730
Over the course
of this year, I'm

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:40.750
sure the NVIDIA
Inference Server will get

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:43.228
more stable and more robust.

00:43:43.228 --> 00:43:45.770
There are some other things to
say about the NVIDIA Inference

00:43:45.770 --> 00:43:48.170
Server.

00:43:48.170 --> 00:43:51.160
It's not dynamic, actually,
yet, like TF serving

00:43:51.160 --> 00:43:52.610
is, which is a problem.

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:56.090
Almost every practical
ML application

00:43:56.090 --> 00:43:58.550
is dynamic in some
sense, meaning,

00:43:58.550 --> 00:44:02.090
you almost never train
a model on static data

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:05.527
and then just serve
it ad infinitum.

00:44:05.527 --> 00:44:07.610
Typically, you're going
to update that model based

00:44:07.610 --> 00:44:08.790
on new data at some point.

00:44:08.790 --> 00:44:12.050
And so the process of
deploying the new model

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:13.280
gets much more complicated.

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:20.030
Because you have to have
your own system for doing

00:44:20.030 --> 00:44:22.040
this hot-swap, or
you have to be able

00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:25.460
bring the whole server down
and then swap in a new model

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:29.670
and bring it back up, or
something more complicated,

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:33.523
where you migrate traffic
over from-- you deploy

00:44:33.523 --> 00:44:35.690
a new instance, and you
migrate-- so anyway, there's

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:37.220
a reason why TF
serving is dynamic,

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:41.270
is because it's actually a pain
when serving the infrastructure

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:42.165
is not dynamic.

00:44:42.165 --> 00:44:43.373
AUDIENCE: So what did you do?

00:44:43.373 --> 00:44:47.221
You said, you changed the
graph and you [INAUDIBLE]..

00:44:47.221 --> 00:44:48.863
So how did you get
to [INAUDIBLE]??

00:44:48.863 --> 00:44:50.530
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Those
other optimizations

00:44:50.530 --> 00:44:52.390
that we performed did
make a difference.

00:44:52.390 --> 00:44:53.730
So we still--

00:44:53.730 --> 00:44:55.030
AUDIENCE: Which ones?

00:44:55.030 --> 00:44:56.560
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Let's go back.

00:44:56.560 --> 00:44:59.185
This is the list that we talked
about that we were going to do.

00:44:59.185 --> 00:45:04.980
So this removing identity
nodes, folding constants,

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:10.230
merging duplicate nodes,
actually, is a form of pruning.

00:45:10.230 --> 00:45:12.690
You have two nodes
that are in succession

00:45:12.690 --> 00:45:15.390
that do the same operation.

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:21.310
[INAUDIBLE] unused nodes,
meaning, any nodes that

00:45:21.310 --> 00:45:23.320
are being calculated
but are not actually

00:45:23.320 --> 00:45:25.150
connected to the output.

00:45:25.150 --> 00:45:27.460
So that would include extra
output nodes, but also,

00:45:27.460 --> 00:45:30.370
just like any-- there are
oftentimes nodes in your graph

00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:34.540
that end up getting
added that are not--

00:45:34.540 --> 00:45:37.220
there's no edge from that
node back to the output,

00:45:37.220 --> 00:45:39.610
and so those nodes get pruned.

00:45:39.610 --> 00:45:41.650
And then this
folding batch norms,

00:45:41.650 --> 00:45:43.840
which we talked about as well.

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:45.940
So that's the set of
transforms that we did.

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:52.900
So this is a good topic.

00:45:52.900 --> 00:45:58.995
This actually leads
me to the conclusion

00:45:58.995 --> 00:46:05.140
of the talk, which is, the
truth is, that grappler, which

00:46:05.140 --> 00:46:07.900
I mentioned, which is built
into the TensorFlow runtime,

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:10.030
is actually performing
all of those optimizations

00:46:10.030 --> 00:46:12.340
that we listed.

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:15.446
And TF serving, which is the
serving mechanism that we used,

00:46:15.446 --> 00:46:17.860
uses grappler.

00:46:17.860 --> 00:46:21.770
But we still see the
results that we see.

00:46:21.770 --> 00:46:24.060
We see them consistently
across multiple models.

00:46:24.060 --> 00:46:28.100
We actually implemented this
using vanilla TensorFlow

00:46:28.100 --> 00:46:32.630
and Keras, and all of that
sample code is in our repo.

00:46:32.630 --> 00:46:36.050
And yet, we see this
performance increase.

00:46:36.050 --> 00:46:39.440
I've been talking to the
folks both on the TF serving

00:46:39.440 --> 00:46:41.810
side and the grappler
side to try to understand

00:46:41.810 --> 00:46:43.140
why this is the case.

00:46:43.140 --> 00:46:45.980
But it highlights
the fact that you

00:46:45.980 --> 00:46:49.260
need to take control
of your serving.

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:52.970
If you care about
performance, you

00:46:52.970 --> 00:46:56.540
should not rely necessarily on
everything working perfectly

00:46:56.540 --> 00:46:57.433
under the hood.

00:46:57.433 --> 00:46:58.850
You would like to,
but the reality

00:46:58.850 --> 00:47:01.460
is is that, for some
reason, and I'm still

00:47:01.460 --> 00:47:05.360
not sure why grappler is
not performing in the way

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:07.680
that it's supposed
to in this context,

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:11.510
but nonetheless, it's
very reproducible.

00:47:11.510 --> 00:47:13.980
For some reason, the
grappler optimizations

00:47:13.980 --> 00:47:16.230
that are supposed to be
happening inside of TensorFlow

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:19.390
serving are not happening.

00:47:19.390 --> 00:47:24.100
So again, in an
ideal world, this set

00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:28.780
of optimizations for most cases,
if you're using TF serving,

00:47:28.780 --> 00:47:30.320
should not be necessary.

00:47:30.320 --> 00:47:33.820
Now, there are some
cases where you're

00:47:33.820 --> 00:47:38.050
doing another kind of serving
mechanism that you can't

00:47:38.050 --> 00:47:40.070
rely on grappler kicking in.

00:47:40.070 --> 00:47:43.223
And so this is a good,
useful knowledge to have.

00:47:43.223 --> 00:47:45.640
But also, the reality is is
that despite grappler supposed

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:49.030
to be performing these
optimizations in the tests

00:47:49.030 --> 00:47:50.510
that we ran, it was not.

00:47:50.510 --> 00:47:54.520
And again, we'll
figure out why that is.

00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:59.830
As you know, TensorFlow is a
very rapidly evolving system

00:47:59.830 --> 00:48:02.870
and things don't
always work perfectly.

00:48:02.870 --> 00:48:07.760
And so you should take control
over your own model process,

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:12.340
particularly if there
could be a 15% difference

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:14.230
in your performance.

00:48:14.230 --> 00:48:17.710
And understanding all of these
processes and how to test

00:48:17.710 --> 00:48:21.600
them is important.

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:25.470
But the reality is,
again, I think--

00:48:25.470 --> 00:48:28.830
we discovered this after we
published the post in November,

00:48:28.830 --> 00:48:31.110
that actually, grappler
is supposed to be applying

00:48:31.110 --> 00:48:32.370
all these optimizations.

00:48:32.370 --> 00:48:35.680
And because TF serving
users grappler,

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:39.370
these optimizations
shouldn't work, but they do.

00:48:39.370 --> 00:48:41.712
And so something's wacky.

00:48:41.712 --> 00:48:44.170
Again, maybe it's something
that we're doing, I don't know.

00:48:44.170 --> 00:48:47.557
But we repeated these,
and like I said,

00:48:47.557 --> 00:48:49.140
we did it on more
than just one model.

00:48:49.140 --> 00:48:51.870
We did it using multiple APIs.

00:48:51.870 --> 00:48:56.140
It was very consistent that we
saw about this 15% improvement.

00:48:56.140 --> 00:48:59.280
So I don't have all
the answers here.

00:48:59.280 --> 00:49:02.050
I don't know exactly
what's going on.

00:49:02.050 --> 00:49:06.030
But anyway, this was, I
think, still a useful exercise

00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:09.840
in understanding the
details of the model

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:13.380
formats and the optimization
processes and things

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:15.540
like quantization,
which we didn't do here,

00:49:15.540 --> 00:49:18.630
but can be a really
important factor

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:21.510
in the efficiency of your
serving processes as well.

00:49:24.240 --> 00:49:26.190
So that's the end here, again.

00:49:26.190 --> 00:49:28.260
We had lots of questions--
really great questions

00:49:28.260 --> 00:49:28.960
during the talk.

00:49:28.960 --> 00:49:30.900
But if anybody else
has any more questions,

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:32.760
I'd be happy to take them now.

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:33.260
Yeah?

00:50:22.778 --> 00:50:25.320
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Like, would what
we're doing here change that?

00:50:25.320 --> 00:50:26.270
Like, maybe--

00:50:40.365 --> 00:50:41.240
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Right.

00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:42.198
That's a good question.

00:50:42.198 --> 00:50:44.510
So the whole topic
of explainability--

00:50:44.510 --> 00:50:46.970
maybe I'll come back someday
and give a talk about that.

00:50:46.970 --> 00:50:48.720
I've actually been
working on that myself.

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:52.240
A very, very hard,
but interesting topic

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:54.110
in the world of ML these days.

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:58.090
Lots of people are
interested in it.

00:50:58.090 --> 00:51:01.900
The short answer is what
we did here should not

00:51:01.900 --> 00:51:07.300
change the mathematical
result of the model, meaning,

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:09.340
none of the
optimizations that we

00:51:09.340 --> 00:51:12.520
did here should change the
mathematical results that

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:13.870
are computed by the graph.

00:51:13.870 --> 00:51:16.690
But again, you
have to test that.

00:51:16.690 --> 00:51:18.160
Don't believe me.

00:51:18.160 --> 00:51:21.090
That's why I said run the
predictions on a test set.

00:51:21.090 --> 00:51:24.520
In the case of MNIST,
it's the MNIST data set.

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:26.050
And then run it
again and make sure

00:51:26.050 --> 00:51:30.910
that your outputs look the
same for a given input.

00:51:30.910 --> 00:51:33.200
This is not that hard to do.

00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:35.590
There's only 60,000 examples
in the MNIST data set.

00:51:35.590 --> 00:51:39.640
So you should really verify
that the numerical output

00:51:39.640 --> 00:51:42.130
for a given input is the same.

00:51:42.130 --> 00:51:44.670
But the truth is, all
the optimizations that

00:51:44.670 --> 00:51:46.420
are performed here
should not have changed

00:51:46.420 --> 00:51:48.300
the mathematical
calculations that are

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:49.720
being performed by the graph.

00:51:49.720 --> 00:51:51.730
Interpretation is
another big topic

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:54.820
that we, unfortunately,
probably don't have time

00:51:54.820 --> 00:51:56.800
to talk about much
today, but maybe I'll

00:51:56.800 --> 00:51:58.300
come back and talk
about that later.

00:51:58.300 --> 00:52:00.580
AUDIENCE: Just one
last [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:00.580 --> 00:52:04.988
How do you create the benchmark
numbers on various [INAUDIBLE]??

00:52:04.988 --> 00:52:08.780
For example, [INAUDIBLE]
35, 34, [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:08.780 --> 00:52:11.752
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yeah,
the absolute values

00:52:11.752 --> 00:52:13.210
of these benchmarks
will definitely

00:52:13.210 --> 00:52:15.935
change according to the model.

00:52:15.935 --> 00:52:17.560
And they will also,
the absolute values

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:20.200
will change depending on
which system you run on.

00:52:20.200 --> 00:52:23.860
But the key thing we're looking
for is, are they consistent--

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:26.050
for a given model
on a given machine,

00:52:26.050 --> 00:52:28.120
are they consistent
from run to run?

00:52:28.120 --> 00:52:32.440
And the method that I
talked about here today

00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:34.970
empirically is quite consistent.

00:52:34.970 --> 00:52:37.170
AUDIENCE: And that was
for less than 34, right?

00:52:37.170 --> 00:52:38.530
LUKMAN RAMSEY: No, no.

00:52:38.530 --> 00:52:41.160
This is just a CNN
that we created

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:45.730
that's just a few layers,
very simple CNN to do MNIST.

00:52:45.730 --> 00:52:46.330
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:52:46.330 --> 00:52:46.730
Thank you.

00:52:46.730 --> 00:52:47.260
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yeah.

00:52:47.260 --> 00:52:47.770
My pleasure.

00:52:47.770 --> 00:52:48.270
No

00:52:48.270 --> 00:52:48.770
problem.

00:52:56.123 --> 00:52:57.165
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Of layers?

00:53:05.030 --> 00:53:05.780
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Oh.

00:53:05.780 --> 00:53:07.405
Yeah, that's a hard
question to answer.

00:53:07.405 --> 00:53:11.160
Yeah, it really depends on
how the model's constructed

00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:13.950
and what operations
are being used.

00:53:13.950 --> 00:53:21.700
These big, deep CNNs that they
use to do image recognition,

00:53:21.700 --> 00:53:24.960
again, like ResNet or Inception,
are actually quite complex

00:53:24.960 --> 00:53:27.930
from an architectural
standpoint if you look down

00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:30.081
in the details of how
they're constructed.

00:53:33.007 --> 00:53:35.090
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Which ones
are compressed the most,

00:53:35.090 --> 00:53:37.482
or which ones--

00:53:37.482 --> 00:53:38.690
yeah, I couldn't answer that.

00:53:41.820 --> 00:53:46.310
I haven't actually done
a comparative survey.

00:53:48.600 --> 00:53:49.100
Yeah?

00:53:52.977 --> 00:53:53.810
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yeah.

00:53:58.840 --> 00:54:00.780
LUKMAN RAMSEY: It should, yes.

00:54:00.780 --> 00:54:02.960
AUDIENCE: Is there
also a [INAUDIBLE]??

00:54:09.430 --> 00:54:10.480
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Oh, no.

00:54:10.480 --> 00:54:12.022
There's nothing that
we could do here

00:54:12.022 --> 00:54:13.840
that would make your
model more accurate.

00:54:13.840 --> 00:54:15.010
There are definitely
things we could

00:54:15.010 --> 00:54:16.120
do that would make it less.

00:54:16.120 --> 00:54:16.780
AUDIENCE: Yes, actually.

00:54:16.780 --> 00:54:17.572
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yes.

00:54:39.408 --> 00:54:40.950
LUKMAN RAMSEY: For
a given accuracy--

00:54:40.950 --> 00:54:43.410
again, if you're
processing medical imagery,

00:54:43.410 --> 00:54:46.110
you probably do care about the
throughput and the predictions

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:46.690
speed.

00:54:46.690 --> 00:54:49.530
Because those are
very big images

00:54:49.530 --> 00:54:52.212
and the models that you're using
probably are quite big as well.

00:54:52.212 --> 00:54:53.670
And so it can make
a big difference

00:54:53.670 --> 00:54:57.030
in the amount of time that
it takes or the costs.

00:54:57.030 --> 00:54:59.473
So these optimizations
make sense, although,

00:54:59.473 --> 00:55:01.140
again, theoretically,
you shouldn't have

00:55:01.140 --> 00:55:02.955
to perform them in many cases.

00:55:08.147 --> 00:55:08.980
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yeah.

00:55:08.980 --> 00:55:11.310
We're actually not here
concerned with optimizing

00:55:11.310 --> 00:55:11.910
accuracy.

00:55:11.910 --> 00:55:14.175
That's the realm of training.

00:55:17.352 --> 00:55:21.270
The whole world of making your
models as accurate as possible

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:24.780
is this world of training,
which people talk about a lot.

00:55:24.780 --> 00:55:26.520
They talk about how
to optimize training

00:55:26.520 --> 00:55:29.280
and how to distribute
training and how

00:55:29.280 --> 00:55:31.140
to make training go fast.

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:32.640
You don't see as
many people talking

00:55:32.640 --> 00:55:34.162
about how to optimize serving.

00:55:34.162 --> 00:55:36.120
That was one of the
motivations that we had for

00:55:36.120 --> 00:55:37.750
doing this work.

00:55:37.750 --> 00:55:38.250
Yeah.

00:55:38.250 --> 00:55:39.975
Thank you.

00:55:39.975 --> 00:55:41.687
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:55:41.687 --> 00:55:42.520
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yeah?

00:55:44.798 --> 00:55:45.590
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yes.

00:55:49.940 --> 00:55:51.810
LUKMAN RAMSEY: There
are coding changes.

00:55:51.810 --> 00:55:53.740
But hopefully, not too invasive.

00:55:53.740 --> 00:55:54.890
Yeah, not too extensive.

00:55:54.890 --> 00:55:56.840
If you're using
the estimator APR,

00:55:56.840 --> 00:56:00.420
then it's actually
pretty minimal.

00:56:00.420 --> 00:56:02.700
And I don't know about Keras
yet with respect to TPUs.

00:56:13.122 --> 00:56:13.872
LUKMAN RAMSEY: OK.

00:56:24.010 --> 00:56:26.120
LUKMAN RAMSEY: That's right.

00:56:26.120 --> 00:56:27.550
Actually, I couldn't
ever believe

00:56:27.550 --> 00:56:31.820
how we would expose to the
outside world a TPU for free.

00:56:31.820 --> 00:56:32.320
I know.

00:56:32.320 --> 00:56:34.910
Somehow we managed to do that.

00:56:34.910 --> 00:56:36.925
But yeah.

00:56:36.925 --> 00:56:38.800
You would figure some
bitcoin miners would've

00:56:38.800 --> 00:56:40.508
figured out how to
take advantage of that

00:56:40.508 --> 00:56:42.385
somehow by now.

00:56:42.385 --> 00:56:42.885
Yeah?

00:56:49.047 --> 00:56:49.880
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Yeah.

00:56:49.880 --> 00:56:53.030
So if you go look on the
documentation for this graph

00:56:53.030 --> 00:56:56.000
transform tool,
there are actually

00:56:56.000 --> 00:56:58.670
lots of parameters and
ways that you can adjust

00:56:58.670 --> 00:57:03.320
or optional transforms
that you can perform

00:57:03.320 --> 00:57:07.285
that may be more aggressive.

00:57:07.285 --> 00:57:09.410
We don't have the time to
talk about all that here.

00:57:09.410 --> 00:57:15.110
But yeah, there are
some more experimental

00:57:15.110 --> 00:57:18.470
transforms as well that can be
performed that have a greater

00:57:18.470 --> 00:57:20.600
likelihood of
changing your results

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:24.350
or potentially not
working with your graph.

00:57:24.350 --> 00:57:26.090
So yeah, this
world of transforms

00:57:26.090 --> 00:57:30.870
is actually quite deep and
rich when you dig into it.

00:57:30.870 --> 00:57:34.880
And again, most of the
time, in most contexts,

00:57:34.880 --> 00:57:38.030
you wouldn't have to
know all this stuff

00:57:38.030 --> 00:57:41.330
because the TensorFlow
runtime is doing it for you.

00:57:41.330 --> 00:57:44.810
But as we saw here,
it's useful to know

00:57:44.810 --> 00:57:46.580
because maybe
something went wrong

00:57:46.580 --> 00:57:48.663
and the TensorFlow runtime
isn't working correctly

00:57:48.663 --> 00:57:51.620
or a TF serving is
not working correctly,

00:57:51.620 --> 00:57:57.040
or that you may not
be using TF serving.

00:57:57.040 --> 00:58:00.530
There are applications that were
in IoT where people are running

00:58:00.530 --> 00:58:03.200
predictions on an edge device,
or even a Raspberry Pi,

00:58:03.200 --> 00:58:05.150
where you're not going
to be able to get

00:58:05.150 --> 00:58:07.730
TF serving on that device.

00:58:07.730 --> 00:58:11.915
And so it's good to know how
to do this kind of stuff.

00:58:11.915 --> 00:58:13.790
Also, just to understand
better, for example,

00:58:13.790 --> 00:58:16.290
what frameworks like TF
Lite, TensorFlow Lite

00:58:16.290 --> 00:58:19.360
are doing under the hood
to optimize your graphs.

00:58:19.360 --> 00:58:23.810
So yeah, it's a very
interesting, deep world,

00:58:23.810 --> 00:58:25.710
most of which is
happening under the hood

00:58:25.710 --> 00:58:27.470
and you know you
don't hear about it.

00:58:27.470 --> 00:58:31.700
But we're hoping to expose
some of this information

00:58:31.700 --> 00:58:35.040
to the public as
part of this work.

00:58:35.040 --> 00:58:35.940
Thank you.

00:58:35.940 --> 00:58:38.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:38.640 --> 00:58:42.890
